3-D resistor mesh 15x15x15, 1k edges, 50k leak per node, corner driven, one operating point
* bench.id: perf_scal_mesh3d_dc_n15
* bench.group: performance
* bench.title: 3-D resistor mesh 15x15x15, 1k edges, 50k leak per node, corner driven, one operating point
* bench.probes: v(n7_7_7), v(n14_14_14), v(n1_0_0), v(n0_0_14), i(v1)
* bench.tol.rel: 1e-6
* bench.tol.abs: 1e-9
* bench.expect: match
* bench.ng.solver: klu
* bench.timing: yes
* bench.size: 3376
* bench.est_rss_mib: 1530
* bench.tags: linear, op, mesh, mesh3d, faer_tier, memory, family:perf_scal_mesh3d_dc
* bench.note: 3376 unknowns (above the 2000-unknown dense oracle limit); scipy sparse LU agrees with both tools at all 3375 nodes to 3.0e-15 V (v(n7_7_7)=0.087734 V, v(n14_14_14)=0.041382 V, i(v1)=-7.8352 mA); measured on an idle core: rustspice e2e 3.45 s (compile 3.41 s, solve 35 ms, VmHWM 1218 MiB = 369 KB per unknown) vs ngspice KLU 148 ms (36 MiB); ngspice SPARSE needs 9.1 s here (4-10x more on a loaded host) and is not run; over the family the rustspice end-to-end exponent is 2.3 against 1.3 for KLU while its solve-only exponent is 1.2, i.e. the symbolic compile and the memory (exponent 2.1, checks/scaling_and_memory_families/check_mem_scaling.py; n=16 needs 1.9 GiB, n=18 4.3 GiB) blow up, not the numeric solve
